# parse_json — turn raw text into `value`s inside a rule body.
#
# Each row of raw.csv carries a kind tag and a payload string. The
# rules below parse each payload via `parse_json`, then exercise the
# resulting `value` against the existing destructuring / projection
# builtins (length, subscript, type_of).
#
# Read the output for what is *missing*. A malformed payload has no
# value, so its whole row is absent rather than present with a NULL
# in the parsed column: the `malformed` row appears in none of the
# four results, not even the default `parsed` one. No NULL appears
# anywhere in this program's output. That is the point worth taking
# away, since a rule that quietly derives fewer tuples than its
# input suggests looks the same as one that derives none for a
# different reason.
#
# To see which payloads were rejected, ask for the ones whose parse
# has no value. `defined` is the readable spelling:
#
#     rejected(P) :- raw(_, P), not defined(parse_json(P)).
#
# `not (e = e)` says the same thing without the builtin, an equality
# needing both sides to have a value:
#
#     rejected(P) :- raw(_, P), not (parse_json(P) = parse_json(P)).
#
# Note on object payloads: SQLite's `json()` validates and minifies
# but does not sort object keys, while Postgres `jsonb` and the
# native evaluator both canonicalise key order. To keep the expected
# output identical across every backend, the object payload here
# uses keys that are already in sorted order. See spec §2.9.

input predicate raw(kind: string, payload: string).

# Parse each payload into a `value`. A malformed payload has no value,
# so the equality does not hold and no tuple is derived for that row.
parsed(K, P, J) :- raw(K, P), J = parse_json(P).

# Length of each parsed value: array length / object key count. Four
# rows, one per row `parsed` kept; the malformed payload never got
# this far.
output predicate parsed_length(K, N) :- parsed(K, _, J), N = length(J).

# Projection on parsed arrays: pull the first element as a `value`,
# then coerce to integer. Two rows out of four, and the two that go
# do so for different reasons: the object misses the array-shape
# gate, while `empty_array` passes it and then loses `J[0]`, a
# missing index having no value.
output predicate first_element(K, V) :-
    parsed(K, _, J),
    type_of(J) = "array",
    V = as_integer(J[0]).

# Type of each parsed value. Three rows rather than four: the two
# arrays agree, and a predicate's extension is a set.
output predicate parsed_type(K, T) :- parsed(K, _, J), T = type_of(J).

?- parsed(K, P, J).
